<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scanian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1675">1675</a>–<a href="/years/1679">1679</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scanian War was fought from 1675 to 1679 between Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, as part of the wider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-dutch-war">Franco-Dutch War</a>. Denmark-Norway invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> to recover the provinces lost in 1658 and won at sea under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-juel">Niels Juel</a>, but lost the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> in 1676. Under French pressure the peace treaties of 1679 restored the pre-war borders.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scanian-war.jpg" alt="Scanian War" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Imonoz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scanian_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> had suffered a financial crisis in the 1660s and early 1670s. In April 1672 it allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, which promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> for a Swedish army in its German dominions and later raised them to 900,000 riksdaler, threatening to withdraw them if the army was not used. In December 1674 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> called on Sweden to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. The Swedish army was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fehrbellin">Battle of Fehrbellin</a> on 28 June 1675, the first such defeat of Swedish forces since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. Christian V of Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, who had hesitated to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> against France, now saw his chance and, overcoming the opposition of his adviser Peder Griffenfeld, attacked.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Danish aim was to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> and the other provinces ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> after the Northern Wars. Christian V took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helsingborg">Helsingborg</a> on 29 June 1676 with 15,000 troops against about 5,000 Swedes, and much of the peasantry sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, so that within a month only Malmö remained Swedish. On the Norwegian front, in the Gyldenløve War, Ulrik Frederick Gyldenløve took Uddevalla and Vänersborg and later Marstrand and regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohuslan">Bohuslän</a>, and Norwegian forces retook Jämtland in 1677.</p><p>In October 1676 the 19-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xi-of-sweden">Charles XI</a> marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> with 12,000 men, and the Danish army was beaten at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> on 4 December. In July 1678 Christian V chose not to give battle at Kristianstad and evacuated his troops from Scania.</p><p>At sea the Danish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet won at Öland on 1 June 1676, sinking the Swedish ship Kronan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niels-juel">Niels Juel</a> won at Fehmarn on 31 May 1677 and again at Køge Bay, where the Swedes lost over 3,000 men to about 375 on the Danish-Norwegian side. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and its allies took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen-verden">Bremen-Verden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Swedish Pomerania</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> falling in December 1677, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a> on 11 October 1678 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> on 5 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> negotiated peace for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the Treaty of Fontainebleau of 23 August 1679, which required that all territory Sweden had lost be returned. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> confirmed it between Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and Sweden, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> received minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> while returning Rügen. Brandenburg returned most of its gains in the Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a> the war raised the hopes of the pro-Danish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> known as Snapphane and their sympathisers, who then suffered harsh repression from the restored Swedish authorities. Scholars disagree about the result: some see a Swedish defeat, others an inconclusive war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scanian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Roskilde in 1658 surrendered Scania, Halland and Blekinge, the rich eastern provinces, to Sweden, and the <b>Scanian War</b> of 1675–1679 failed to win them back.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>A Swedish army of about 8,000 under Charles XI defeated a Danish army of some 13,000 under Christian V north of Lund in Scania, in the <b>Scanian War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-4">4 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
